data.content:
Since there's no project file to check the target framework, here's the lightest option:

**Use `TimeProvider` (.NET 8+)** — it's built-in, no new interfaces needed:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (one NuGet package):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-10)); // can rewind too
```

**Why this is the lightest:**
- No custom interface/wrapper to maintain
- The `= null` default means existing callers don't break (no DI container required)
- `TimeProvider` is in the BCL — zero production dependencies

If you're on **.NET 6/7**, the equivalent is a one-line `Func<DateTimeOffset>` parameter with the same optional-default trick. Want me to apply the `TimeProvider` change to your file?
